# CASP15 H1135 — The Nuclear Bridge (7Z8Y)
---

Every cell in your body has a nucleus,
and that nucleus needs to be physically
connected to the rest of the cell.

This is the SUN1-KASH6 complex — a molecular
bridge that spans the nuclear envelope.

Published in Communications Biology in 2024.

> close
> load 7z8y
---

This complex is part of the LINC system —
Linker of Nucleoskeleton and Cytoskeleton.

It literally connects the DNA-organizing scaffold
inside the nucleus to the structural framework
outside it. Without this bridge, cells can't
properly position their nuclei.

> spin 0.15
---

What's remarkable about this structure is its
asymmetry. Most people assumed LINC complexes
were symmetric, but this crystal structure
revealed an unexpected asymmetric architecture.

That asymmetry matters — it explains how the
complex inserts into the double membrane
of the nuclear envelope.

> stop
> view ligand
---

Mutations in LINC complex proteins cause
devastating diseases — muscular dystrophy,
cardiomyopathy, and premature aging syndromes
like progeria.

Understanding this bridge at atomic resolution
is a step toward treating these diseases.

> reset
> spin 0.1
---

The SUN1-KASH6 complex — the bridge that holds
your nucleus in place.
PDB code 7Z8Y, CASP15 target H1135.

